{{right|''Dryden.''}}}}

It was sometimes used elliptically for ''this is.''

{{smaller block|''This'' 'a good Fryer, belike.
{{right|''Shak.'', M. for M. (folio 1623), v. 1. 131.}}}}

'''From this out.''' See ''from.''—'''To put this and that together.''' See ''put''<sup>1</sup>.

'''this''' (ᴛ̵ʜis), ''adv.'' [A var. of ''thus'', or an elliptical use of ''for this.'' Cf. ''that, adv.''] For this; thus. [Obsolete or colloq.]

{{smaller block|What am I, that thou shouldst contemn me ''this?''
{{right|''Shak.'', Venus and Adonis, l. 205.}}}}

{{smaller block|None of the portraits mentioned by Walpole . . . are dated ''this'' early.
{{right|''J. P. Norris'', in Shakespeariana, May, 1884, p. 181.}}}}

'''thisbe''' (thiz′bē), ''n.'' [< NL. ''thisbe'', the specific name, < Gr. Θίσβη, a proper name.] The clear-winged moth ''Hemaris thisbe.''

